===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# An [[advertisement]] in a common [[media]] format, usually [[radio]] or [[television]].
#: {{ux|en|She was in a '''commercial''' for breakfast cereal.}}
# {{lb|en|finance}} A commercial [[trader]], as opposed to an [[individual]] [[speculator]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[commercial traveller]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1875|author=George Worsley|title=Advice to the Young!|page=32
|passage=I have more than once had to lend a '''commercial''' money to pay his fare home; as he had played shell-out and lost the lot.}}
#* {{RQ:Marsh Beetle|passage=Five persons went to the house after the milkman was gone, and that there Arab party was safe inside, — three of them was '''commercials''', that I know, because afterwards they came to me.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1911|author={{w|Steele Rudd|‘Steel Rudd’}}|title=The Book of Dan|location=Brisbane|publisher=University of Queensland Press|year_published=1970|page=84|passage=‘I’ve seen him take things and lift them into a dray - things that - that - that - well, that you and me together couldn’t lift an inch!’ ‘Go on!’ and the '''commercial''' again squinted in the direction of Dan.}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[male]] [[prostitute]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1972|author=Alfred Eustace Parker|title=The Berkeley Police Story|page=133
|passage=Tom said that homosexuals hate “'''commercials''',” male prostitutes, and if the homosexual was drunk and angry, he might have committed murder.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1987|author=Paul William Mathews|title=Male Prostitution: Two Monographs|page=39
|passage=With the '''commercials''' there is no intensity of feeling and no later animosity; there is emotional and sexual fakery, but no prolonged post-sexual bargaining. {{...}} Paradoxically these boys dissociate themselves from the '''commercials''', yet engage in prostitution only when they require the money.}}

